1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,800
Hey, my name is Andrew, and in this video I want to talk about using your

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:08,480
own voice with Lyceum.

3
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:16,520
A synthetic voice is great when you need a video fast, or you'd rather not record

4
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:17,080
yourself.

5
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:23,020
But when the video is yours to present, a talk, a demo,

6
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:28,580
an update for your team, people listen differently to someone they know.

7
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:31,680
So this time, you're hearing me.

8
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:39,500
I read this script out loud, once, and Lyceum timed every animation to my words.

9
00:00:41,850 --> 00:00:45,310
Reading a script out loud is harder than it sounds.

10
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:50,610
Long sentences run out of breath, and the words start to sound read.

11
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:55,350
So I wrote this as short lines, like bullet points.

12
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:59,570
Each line is one breath, and I pause at the end of it.

13
00:00:59,890 --> 00:01:05,010
I'm still reading every word, but it sounds less like reading, and a little bit more

14
00:01:05,010 --> 00:01:05,850
like talking.

15
00:01:08,217 --> 00:01:12,897
Lyceum puts each element on screen at the moment its words are spoken.

16
00:01:14,057 --> 00:01:17,397
A synthetic voice tells it exactly when that is.

17
00:01:17,797 --> 00:01:19,317
My voice doesn't.

18
00:01:20,077 --> 00:01:23,437
I speed up, I slow down, I take a breath.

19
00:01:24,237 --> 00:01:29,937
So Lyceum listens to my take, and finds every word of the script in it.

20
00:01:30,617 --> 00:01:35,437
Wherever I said a word, that's where its animation lands.

21
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:40,367
I record the whole talk in one go.

22
00:01:40,807 --> 00:01:44,687
Recording scene by scene would sound stitched together.

23
00:01:45,467 --> 00:01:49,807
Lyceum cuts the take into scenes, in the pauses between them.

24
00:01:50,307 --> 00:01:56,927
Anything I say between scenes, like next slide, falls in a gap and is dropped.

25
00:01:57,547 --> 00:02:04,547
Each scene is trimmed, the room noise is cleaned out, and every scene is brought to

26
00:02:04,547 --> 00:02:05,407
the same volume.

27
00:02:07,783 --> 00:02:10,363
Nobody reads two minutes without a slip.

28
00:02:10,563 --> 00:02:15,503
If I stumble, Lyceum tells me which scene, and which words it didn't hear.

29
00:02:15,703 --> 00:02:20,463
I record just that scene again, and it replaces that part of the take.

30
00:02:20,963 --> 00:02:23,243
Everything else stays as it was.

31
00:02:23,603 --> 00:02:27,783
And if I say something the script doesn't have, it keeps the audio,

32
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:31,183
but warns me, because the captions won't show it.

33
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:40,020
To narrate a video yourself, write the script as short lines, one per breath,

34
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:44,360
and add one line at the top, saying the voice is recorded.

35
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:48,920
Record the whole talk into the recordings folder.

36
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:54,060
Then run narrate, just as you would with a synthetic voice.

37
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:59,200
This video started as one take.

38
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:03,720
Everything you just watched is timed to it.
